The '''Occidental Agreement of 1846''' was the settlement that returned Sarta's coastal territories to the kingdom after decades of dispute. The agreement restored the lands as [[Occidental Province]] and secured a 50% discount on coal exports to [[Volisania]] for the following 99 years. Anti-Volisanian critics later called the agreement the '''Tontas Trato''' or "foolish deal", but the formal name of the settlement was the Occidental Agreement.

== Background ==

By the mid-nineteenth century, pressure had grown in Sarta for the return of the coastal territories held by Volisania. Domestic opinion favoured recovery of the lost coast, but most of the political elite remained cautious after the civil wars of the previous decades. The kingdom also faced outside pressure from [[Jacan]] and [[Echia]], both of which wanted a stable balance in Illypnia and feared that renewed fragmentation would strengthen [[Fosian]] powers.

The result was a negotiation shaped by caution as much as by ambition. Sartan lords wanted the coast back, but they also wanted to avoid a new war. Volisania, meanwhile, was willing to bargain so long as it retained an economic advantage and preserved its coal supply.

== Terms ==

Under the agreement, the coastal territories were returned to Sarta and reorganised as [[Occidental Province]]. In exchange, Sarta granted Volisania a 50% discount on coal exports for 99 years. The arrangement gave Volisania an inexpensive supply of coal while allowing Sarta to recover the coastal lands without another military campaign.

The agreement was framed as a practical compromise by its supporters. Critics argued that it sacrificed too much future revenue and accepted too much Volisanian leverage over the kingdom's economy. The debate over the agreement remained active long after the territorial transfer was completed.

== Significance ==

The Occidental Agreement ended the postwar dispute over the coast and marked a shift in Sartan politics from territorial loss to economic dependence. It also deepened the distinction between the pro-Volisania political current and those who favoured a more independent line. Even after the coast was returned, Sarta remained tied to Volisania through coal trade and diplomacy.

The agreement is one of the defining settlements of nineteenth-century Sarta, since it restored territory while also confirming the kingdom's limited room for manoeuvre in regional politics.

